{{smaller block|Any silk, any thread,<br>Any ''toys'' for your head?
{{right|''Shak.'', W. T., iv. 4. 326.}}}}

{{smaller block|One cannot but be amazed to see such a profusion of wealth laid out in coaches, trappings, tables, cabinets, and the like precious ''toys'', in which there are few princes in Europe who equal them.
{{right|''Addison'', Remarks on Italy (Works, ed. Bohn, I. 504).}}}}

2. Something intended rather for amusement than for serious use; a means of diversion: hence, especially, an object contrived or used occasionally for the amusement of children or others; a plaything; also, something diminutive, like a plaything.

{{smaller block|'Tis a pretty ''toy'' to be a poet.
{{right|''Marlowe'', Tamburlaine, I., ii. 2.}}}}

{{smaller block|O virtue, virtue! what art thou become,<br>That man should leave thee for that ''toy'', a woman!
{{right|''Dryden'', Spanish Friar, iv. 2.}}}}

{{smaller block|All the world I saw or knew<br>Seemed a complex Chinese ''toy'',<br>Fashioned for a barefoot boy!
{{right|''Whittier'', Barefoot Boy.}}}}

{{smaller block|Perched on the top of a hill was a conspicuous ''toy'' of a church.
{{right|''W. Black'', House-boat, ii.}}}}

3. A trifle; a thing or matter of no importance or value.

{{smaller block|A man whose wisdom is in weighty affairs admired would take it in some disdain to have his counsel solemnly asked about a ''toy''.
{{right|''Hooker'', Eccles. Polity, i. 15.}}}}

{{smaller block|A ''toy'', a thing of no regard.
{{right|''Shak.'', 1 Hen. VI., iv. 1. 145.}}}}

4. Play; amorous sport; caress.

{{smaller block|So said he, and forbore not glance or ''toy''<br>Of amorous intent.
{{right|''Milton'', P. L., ix. 1034.}}}}
